From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hefesto DualSense4Unix Project <hefesto-dualsense4unix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] HID: playstation: do not parse microphone audio frames as
 gamepad input

When the DualSense microphone is streaming over Bluetooth, the firmware sends
the Opus audio frames using the SAME report id (0x31), the SAME 78 byte size
and a valid CRC-32 as a regular input report. Only bit 1 of byte 1 tells them
apart, and the driver never looks at it.

So the driver parses compressed audio as gamepad state. Two things follow, and
both were measured on a Pop!_OS 24.04 machine with a DualSense over Bluetooth:

  1. ds_report->buttons[2] lands on audio payload, so DS_BUTTONS2_MIC_MUTE
     toggles with the bitstream. On each rising edge the driver flips
     ds->mic_muted and schedules the work that writes
     DS_OUTPUT_POWER_SAVE_CONTROL_MIC_MUTE -- the driver turns the user's
     microphone off by itself. Measured: capture stops after ~1.1 s and never
     comes back, because once muted the firmware stops streaming and no further
     edges are produced.

  2. The axes and the remaining buttons receive audio values, so the pointer
     and the keyboard move on their own. Userspace sees stick deflection and
     key events that nobody produced.

Measured before and after loading the patched module, same instrument and same
duration, with the microphone on: dwell times of the MicMuted bit went from
1231 transitions (6 ms median) to a single one, with the bit steady for 152 s.
Both symptoms are gone.

Discard those reports: an audio frame carries no input state, so there is
nothing to publish. Return 0 rather than -EILSEQ -- the report is not
malformed, it is simply of another kind, and erroring out would flood dmesg at
about a hundred lines per second while the microphone is on.

Note this only affects the driver's own parsing. hidraw consumers still receive
the full report, so userspace bridges that decode the microphone keep working.

Signed-off-by: Hefesto DualSense4Unix Project <hefesto-dualsense4unix@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c | 36 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c b/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-playstation.c
@@ -141,6 +141,13 @@
 #define DS_INPUT_REPORT_USB_SIZE		64
 #define DS_INPUT_REPORT_BT			0x31
 #define DS_INPUT_REPORT_BT_SIZE			78
+
+/*
+ * HEFESTO / MIC-NAO-E-BOTAO-01: bit 1 do byte 1 de um 0x31 de Bluetooth.
+ * Ligado, o report carrega quadros de audio Opus do microfone -- nao estado
+ * de gamepad. Mesmo id, mesmo tamanho e CRC valido nos dois casos.
+ */
+#define DS_INPUT_BT_FLAG_AUDIO 0x02
 #define DS_OUTPUT_REPORT_USB			0x02
 #define DS_OUTPUT_REPORT_USB_SIZE		63
 #define DS_OUTPUT_REPORT_BT			0x31
@@ -1589,6 +1596,37 @@
 			return -EILSEQ;
 		}
 
+		/*
+		 * HEFESTO / MIC-NAO-E-BOTAO-01 (10/09/2026): quando o microfone
+		 * do controle esta no ar, o firmware manda os quadros de audio
+		 * Opus com o MESMO reportID 0x31, o MESMO tamanho de 78 bytes e
+		 * um CRC-32 valido. Nada acima distingue os dois -- so o bit 1
+		 * do byte 1, que o firmware liga nos reports de AUDIO.
+		 *
+		 * Sem esta guarda o driver le audio comprimido como estado de
+		 * gamepad. As consequencias sao duas, e as duas foram medidas:
+		 *
+		 *  1. `buttons[2]` cai sobre payload de audio, e o bit
+		 *     DS_BUTTONS2_MIC_MUTE oscila. Na borda de subida o driver
+		 *     inverte ds->mic_muted e agenda o trabalho que escreve
+		 *     POWER_SAVE_CONTROL_MIC_MUTE -- ou seja, DESLIGA O
+		 *     MICROFONE sozinho, sem ninguem encostar no controle;
+		 *  2. os eixos e os botoes recebem valores de audio, e o cursor
+		 *     e o teclado do usuario se mexem sozinhos.
+		 *
+		 * A metade em espaco de usuario deste projeto ja fazia a mesma
+		 * guarda desde 16/08/2026 -- ver INPUT_FLAG_AUDIO em
+		 * src/hefesto_dualsense4unix/core/physical_report_reader.py.
+		 * Faltava aqui, que e onde o evdev nasce.
+		 *
+		 * Descartar e o certo: um report de audio nao carrega estado de
+		 * input nenhum, entao nao ha nada a publicar. Devolver 0 diz
+		 * "consumido, sem erro" -- -EILSEQ encheria o dmesg dela a 100
+		 * reports por segundo com o microfone ligado.
+		 */
+		if (data[1] & DS_INPUT_BT_FLAG_AUDIO)
+			return 0;
+
 		ds_report = (struct dualsense_input_report *)&data[2];
 	} else {
 		hid_err(hdev, "Unhandled reportID=%d\n", report->id);
-- 
